# smoke-test.sh configuration for terraform-azurerm-n8n.
# Copy this file to .env and fill in your values.
# .env is gitignored — never commit your API key.

# ── Terraform directory ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Path to the Terraform directory to read state from.
# Defaults to the current working directory, so running the script from inside
# examples/small (or your own root configuration) just works without setting
# this. Override if running the script from a different location.
# TERRAFORM_DIR=/path/to/examples/small

# ── Auto-detected from Terraform (override here if needed) ────────────────────
# These are read automatically from `terraform output` when TERRAFORM_DIR has
# a terraform.tfstate. Only set them here to explicitly override.

# Base URL of your n8n deployment (no trailing slash). Read from
# `terraform output -raw n8n_url` by default.
# N8N_URL=https://n8n.example.com

# Kubernetes namespace n8n is deployed into. Read from
# `terraform output -raw n8n_namespace` by default.
# NAMESPACE=n8n

# AKS cluster + RG (used by `az aks get-credentials` to populate kubectl).
# Read from `terraform output -raw aks_cluster_name` /
# `terraform output -raw aks_resource_group` by default.
# AKS_CLUSTER_NAME=my-aks
# AKS_RESOURCE_GROUP=my-rg

# PostgreSQL FQDN, cross-checked against the main pod's DB_POSTGRESDB_HOST.
# Read from `terraform output -raw postgres_fqdn` by default.
# POSTGRES_FQDN=my-server.postgres.database.azure.com

# Storage account + Blob container, cross-checked against the main pod's
# N8N_EXTERNAL_STORAGE_AZURE_* environment (only when the binary-data mode
# is "azure"). Read from `terraform output -raw storage_account_name` /
# `terraform output -raw azure_blob_container_name` by default.
# STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME=myn8nstorage
# BLOB_CONTAINER_NAME=n8n-binary-data

# ── Required for API and workflow execution tests ─────────────────────────────
# n8n API key — create one in n8n: Settings → API → Create API Key. Without
# this, the API connectivity, workflow execution, and load-scaling checks
# are skipped with a warning.
N8N_API_KEY=your-api-key-here

# ── DNS-not-delegated workaround ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# When the public DNS zone the example creates hasn't been delegated at the
# registrar yet (the typical first-apply state), `n8n_url` won't resolve
# from your machine. Set this to the App Gateway's public IP and the
# script will use `curl --resolve` so HTTPS / API / webhook tests still
# work end-to-end against the AGW listener. Read from
# `terraform output -raw appgw_public_ip` by default; only set explicitly
# if you're running against a deployment whose state you don't have local
# access to.
# APPGW_PUBLIC_IP=20.160.15.211

# ── Optional load-scaling test ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set to "true" to fire CPU-burning workflow executions and confirm KEDA
# scales workers up against Azure Cache for Redis queue depth. Off by
# default — the test creates real load and is opt-in.
# LOAD_TEST=false

# Number of webhook executions to fire during the load test (default: 100).
# LOAD_REQUESTS=100

# How many concurrent in-flight webhook calls (default: 20).
# LOAD_CONCURRENCY=20

# How many seed jobs to queue in phase 1 to trigger the autoscaler before
# queueing the rest (default: 40). The default is calibrated to clear the
# chart's stock `queueMode.workerConcurrency=10` * min replicas (2) = 20
# in-flight slots AND push at least 10 jobs into `bull:jobs:wait` (the
# KEDA trigger threshold). Lower only if the chart-level concurrency or
# the KEDA target list-length have been overridden.
# LOAD_SEED_JOBS=40

# How long each worker job burns CPU in seconds (default: 15). Long
# enough that all 40 seed jobs don't drain before KEDA's first poll cycle.
# LOAD_JOB_DURATION_SECS=15

# Seconds to wait for the autoscaler to react after load is applied
# (default: 240). KEDA's stabilization window means scaling won't appear
# instantly — the default is enough headroom for the queue-depth scaler
# to fire on a cold cache.
# SCALE_WAIT_SECS=240

# ── verify-custom-image.sh only ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# These are ignored by smoke-test.sh. See README.md → "Custom image
# verification" for what each one enables.

# Path to a workflow JSON that uses one of your baked nodes. Enables the
# end-to-end execution check, which is the only thing that proves a *worker*
# resolved the custom node type rather than just the main process. The
# workflow needs a webhook trigger and a node whose type starts with
# "CUSTOM.".
# CUSTOM_NODE_WORKFLOW=./my-node-test.json

# Assert the deployed image repository matches n8n_image_repository exactly.
# EXPECT_IMAGE_REPOSITORY=myregistry.azurecr.io/n8n

# Assert N8N_CUSTOM_EXTENSIONS matches n8n_custom_extensions_path exactly.
# EXPECT_EXTENSIONS_PATH=/opt/n8n-nodes
